package lann:webcrypto@0.1.0;

/// Shared structural types for the WebCrypto-style interfaces.
///
/// Every non-resource type used by the package lives here. Structural types
/// carry no host-side identity, so a single composition can freely share these
/// definitions across multiple components. The stateful objects — keys and
/// in-progress computations — live in the per-primitive-kind interfaces
/// (`mac`, `aead`, …), since a resource is always owned by the one component
/// that implements it.
interface types {
    /// Errors surfaced by key creation and cryptographic operations.
    ///
    /// Failures that indicate misuse of the API are unrepresentable by
    /// construction — operations are one-shot calls on immutable key
    /// resources, so there is no state to misuse — and so have no variant
    /// here.
    variant error {
        /// The supplied key material is invalid for the algorithm (for
        /// example, a wrong-length raw key, or one rejected by an
        /// implementation's key-length policy). The string is
        /// human-readable.
        invalid-key(string),
        /// The supplied nonce is invalid for the algorithm (for example, a
        /// wrong-length AES-GCM nonce). The string is human-readable.
        invalid-nonce(string),
        /// Verification failed: the MAC tag, or the ciphertext or its
        /// associated data, did not verify under the key. Deliberately
        /// carries no detail, so implementations cannot leak *why*
        /// verification failed.
        authentication-failed,
        /// The key was created with `extractable` false, so its material
        /// cannot be exported.
        not-extractable,
        /// The request was well-formed, but this implementation does not
        /// serve the requested algorithm parameters (for example, a key
        /// size the platform's crypto API declines). The string is
        /// human-readable.
        unsupported(string),
        /// The key's nonce budget is exhausted: the implementation can no
        /// longer guarantee nonce uniqueness for this key (for example, an
        /// AES-GCM internal-nonce key reaching SP 800-38D's
        /// 2^32-invocation bound for the RBG-based IV construction). The
        /// key remains valid for `open`; mint a fresh key to continue
        /// sealing.
        key-exhausted,
        /// An implementation-specific operational failure — for example, an
        /// implementation backed by an external keystore (an HSM, a platform
        /// key service) that cannot complete the operation right now, or an
        /// input exceeding a resource limit the implementation enforces
        /// (implementations MAY bound how much input one operation may
        /// buffer; the stream is still fully drained first, per the drain
        /// rule). The string is human-readable. API misuse remains
        /// unrepresentable by construction and never appears here.
        other(string),
    }
}

/// The `mac` primitive kind: message-authentication codes.
///
/// This interface is algorithm-agnostic. Keys are minted by per-algorithm
/// interfaces (such as `hmac-sha2`) and returned as `mac-key` resources bound to
/// their algorithm at creation; every operation then hangs off the key, so a
/// key can never be used with the wrong algorithm.
///
/// A component whose world imports only this interface can *use* any
/// `mac-key` handle it is granted but cannot mint or import keys; only a
/// world importing a key-minting interface (such as `hmac-sha2`) can create them.
/// Key handles are therefore capabilities.
interface mac {
    use types.{error};

    /// A MAC key: an unforgeable capability, bound to one algorithm at
    /// creation.
    ///
    /// `sign`/`verify` are one-shot and stateless per call, mirroring
    /// `crypto.subtle.sign`/`verify`: there is deliberately no incremental
    /// computation resource, so misuse of in-progress state (concurrent
    /// updates, use after finalization) is unrepresentable. The byte
    /// `stream` is the only ingestion path and also the incremental one —
    /// implementations may fold each chunk into the computation as it
    /// arrives or buffer the stream whole; the result is chunking-invariant
    /// either way.
    ///
    /// Warning on truncating producers (applies to every stream-taking
    /// operation in this package): dropping the writer is the stream's only
    /// end-of-input signal and carries no verdict, so a producer that fails
    /// midway is indistinguishable from one that finished — the operation
    /// correctly computes over the delivered prefix. A bare `stream` is
    /// never sufficient input for a fallible producer: when the write path
    /// can fail independently of the party consuming the result (for
    /// example, a writable end forwarded to another component), convey
    /// completeness in-band (e.g. length framing) or discard the result on
    /// producer failure.
    ///
    /// Making progress (applies to every stream-taking operation in this
    /// package): an implementation holds each in-flight operation's bytes,
    /// so it necessarily bounds how many it will service at once — by
    /// leaving a call waiting to start, which the component model already
    /// defines and which needs nothing from this interface. A caller with
    /// several operations in flight must therefore *make progress on all of
    /// them concurrently*, in both directions:
    ///
    /// - feed each operation's input stream without waiting on any other
    ///   operation, and
    /// - drain each operation's returned stream as it becomes available,
    ///   rather than deferring every read until the last call has returned.
    ///
    /// A caller that withholds one in-flight operation's input or output
    /// while awaiting another can deadlock against the implementation's
    /// bound, and no implementation can rescue it: the bytes it is waiting
    /// to reclaim are the ones the caller is holding. The natural shape is
    /// safe — await an operation and drain its stream in the same task,
    /// then run those tasks concurrently. Deferring all draining until
    /// every call has returned is the shape that is not.
    ///
    /// A returned stream must be read to completion or dropped; an
    /// implementation may hold resources until one of the two happens.
    /// Dropping it is always available and always sufficient — a caller that
    /// no longer wants the result never has to drain it to release the
    /// implementation.
    ///
    /// Note on extractability: `extractable` is an API property, not a
    /// physical one — the *implementation* of this interface necessarily
    /// holds the key material. The guarantee is that components holding only
    /// the handle cannot obtain the material through this API.
    ///
    /// Every key resource carrying an extractability gate also exposes it as
    /// a getter, so a holder can ask the question without taking the answer:
    /// interrogating it through `export-key` alone would hand the caller the
    /// material whenever the answer is "yes". The getter is not redundant
    /// with the `extractable` argument the minting interfaces take — a key
    /// resource need not have been minted by the component holding it.
    ///
    /// Getter conventions (package-wide): `algorithm-*` getters project the
    /// W3C Web Cryptography API registry's algorithm properties, spelled
    /// and denominated (bits) as the registry defines them; `*-size`
    /// getters report operation-contract quantities in bytes.
    resource mac-key {
        /// Compute the authentication tag over an entire byte stream keyed
        /// by this key. Resolves once the stream is fully drained (its
        /// writer dropped).
        ///
        /// Fails only for operational reasons (`error.other`, e.g. an
        /// external keystore backing this key that cannot complete the
        /// operation) — never for misuse, which is unrepresentable.
        sign: async func(data: stream<u8>) -> result<list<u8>, error>;

        /// Verify `tag` against the tag computed over an entire byte stream
        /// keyed by this key, in constant time. Like `sign`, resolves only
        /// after the stream is fully drained — even when verification fails,
        /// so a caller feeding the stream concurrently never observes its
        /// writer failing instead of the error.
        ///
        /// Fails with `error.authentication-failed` if the tag does not
        /// verify (the same fail-closed shape as `aead-key.open`, and
        /// deliberately not a `bool`: an ignored boolean fails open, a
        /// dropped `result` does not).
        verify: async func(data: stream<u8>, tag: list<u8>) -> result<_, error>;

        /// The registry name of the algorithm family this key is bound to,
        /// e.g. `"HMAC"` (WebCrypto's `KeyAlgorithm.name`, spelled as the
        /// W3C Web Cryptography API algorithm registry spells it).
        ///
        /// The algorithm's parameters are separate getters
        /// (`algorithm-hash`, `algorithm-length`), so future parameters are
        /// new getters rather than changes to a shared type.
        algorithm-name: func() -> string;

        /// The registry name of the digest this key's algorithm is
        /// parameterized over, if any — e.g. `"SHA-256"` for HMAC-SHA-256
        /// (WebCrypto's `HmacKeyAlgorithm.hash`). `none` for MAC algorithms
        /// not built on a digest.
        algorithm-hash: func() -> option<string>;

        /// The key length in bits (WebCrypto's `HmacKeyAlgorithm.length`:
        /// the length of the key material).
        algorithm-length: func() -> u32;

        /// Whether the key material may be exported.
        extractable: func() -> bool;

        /// The raw key material. Fails with `error.not-extractable` unless
        /// the key was created with `extractable` true.
        export-key: async func() -> result<list<u8>, error>;
    }
}

/// The `aead` primitive kind: single-message authenticated encryption with
/// associated data.
///
/// This is deliberately a *single-message* primitive, mirroring
/// `crypto.subtle.encrypt`/`decrypt`: one nonce, one tag, and memory
/// proportional to the message somewhere in the implementation. Streaming
/// *transport* is still used for the bulk data paths — which lets the
/// plaintext or ciphertext live outside the caller's linear memory — but
/// `open` never releases unverified plaintext, so it necessarily buffers the
/// whole message internally before its result resolves. Content too large
/// for that contract belongs to a future segmented `stream-aead` primitive
/// kind, not to a relaxation of this one.
///
/// `seal`/`open` are stateless per call, so there is no separate computation
/// resource: the key resource carries the operations directly.
interface aead {
    use types.{error};

    /// An AEAD key: an unforgeable capability, bound to one algorithm at
    /// creation. See `mac-key` for the capability, extractability,
    /// truncating-producer and making-progress notes, which apply
    /// identically here.
    resource aead-key {
        /// Encrypt and authenticate `plaintext` under `nonce` with the
        /// associated data `aad`.
        ///
        /// The returned stream carries the ciphertext followed by the
        /// authentication tag (the `crypto.subtle.encrypt` wire format).
        /// Implementations may produce it incrementally (tag last) or buffer
        /// and emit it whole; `async` here only *allows* blocking before the
        /// stream is returned. A caller must therefore drain the returned
        /// stream concurrently with feeding `plaintext` (see `mac-key`'s
        /// making-progress note): an incremental producer may block on the
        /// unread output before it has taken the whole input.
        ///
        /// The caller is responsible for nonce uniqueness per key; nonce
        /// misuse defeats the algorithm's guarantees.
        ///
        /// The plaintext stream is fully drained even when the result is an
        /// error, so a caller feeding the stream concurrently never observes
        /// its writer failing instead of the error.
        seal: async func(nonce: list<u8>, aad: list<u8>, plaintext: stream<u8>) -> result<stream<u8>, error>;

        /// Decrypt and verify `ciphertext` (ciphertext followed by tag, as
        /// produced by `seal`) under `nonce` and `aad`.
        ///
        /// Resolves only after the ciphertext stream is fully drained and the
        /// tag verified: `ok(stream)` *is* the authentication statement, and
        /// unverified plaintext is never observable. Fails with
        /// `error.authentication-failed` if verification fails.
        ///
        /// Like `seal`, the ciphertext stream is fully drained even when the
        /// result is an error.
        open: async func(nonce: list<u8>, aad: list<u8>, ciphertext: stream<u8>) -> result<stream<u8>, error>;

        /// The registry name of the algorithm family this key is bound to,
        /// e.g. `"AES-GCM"` (WebCrypto's `KeyAlgorithm.name`, spelled as
        /// the W3C Web Cryptography API algorithm registry spells it).
        ///
        /// The algorithm's parameters are separate getters (each kind
        /// exposes only the ones its algorithms define), so future
        /// parameters are new getters rather than changes to a shared type.
        algorithm-name: func() -> string;

        /// The key length in bits, e.g. `256` for AES-256-GCM (WebCrypto's
        /// `AesKeyAlgorithm.length`).
        algorithm-length: func() -> u32;

        /// The size in bytes of the nonce this key's `seal`/`open` require,
        /// e.g. `12` for AES-GCM — so a component granted only the key
        /// handle can construct nonces without matching on
        /// `algorithm-name`.
        nonce-size: func() -> u32;

        /// The size in bytes of the authentication tag trailing the
        /// ciphertext in `seal`'s output, e.g. `16` — for framing
        /// arithmetic (sealed length = plaintext length + `tag-size`).
        tag-size: func() -> u32;

        /// Whether the key material may be exported. See `mac-key`.
        extractable: func() -> bool;

        /// The raw key material. Fails with `error.not-extractable` unless
        /// the key was created with `extractable` true.
        export-key: async func() -> result<list<u8>, error>;
    }
}

/// The `aead-internal-nonce` primitive kind: single-message authenticated
/// encryption with the nonce generated within the implementation (SP
/// 800-38D §8's "IV generated within the cryptographic module").
///
/// This is the misuse-resistant counterpart of `aead`: the nonce never
/// appears in the API, so nonce reuse — the most common catastrophic AEAD
/// failure — is unrepresentable rather than merely discouraged. Each `seal`
/// draws a fresh nonce via a construction that guarantees per-key
/// uniqueness and carries it in the sealed output, making every sealed
/// message self-contained.
///
/// Keys are a distinct resource from `aead.aead-key`, minted by separate
/// per-algorithm interfaces: the nonce discipline is part of the key
/// capability, so one key can never mix implementation-chosen and
/// caller-chosen nonces. Prefer this kind unless interop requires an
/// externally specified nonce layout; among its algorithms, prefer
/// XChaCha20-Poly1305 for high-volume keys (see the minting interfaces'
/// nonce-budget notes).
///
/// Like `aead`, this is a single-message primitive: `open` buffers and
/// verifies before releasing any plaintext, and both operations fully drain
/// their input streams even when the result is an error. See `mac-key` for
/// the capability, extractability, truncating-producer and making-progress
/// notes, which apply identically here.
interface aead-internal-nonce {
    use types.{error};

    /// An internal-nonce AEAD key: an unforgeable capability, bound to one
    /// algorithm at creation.
    resource internal-nonce-key {
        /// Encrypt and authenticate `plaintext` under a fresh
        /// implementation-generated nonce with the associated data `aad`.
        ///
        /// The returned stream is a self-contained sealed message:
        /// everything `open` needs besides the key and `aad` is carried
        /// in-band. The wire format is documented by the key's minting
        /// interface. As with `aead-key.seal`, implementations may produce
        /// it incrementally or buffer and emit it whole, so a caller must
        /// drain the returned stream concurrently with feeding `plaintext`
        /// (see `mac-key`'s making-progress note).
        ///
        /// Fails with `error.key-exhausted` once the implementation can no
        /// longer guarantee nonce uniqueness for this key.
        seal: async func(aad: list<u8>, plaintext: stream<u8>) -> result<stream<u8>, error>;

        /// Decrypt and verify a sealed message, as produced by `seal` under
        /// the same algorithm, with the associated data `aad`.
        ///
        /// Resolves only after the stream is fully drained and the tag
        /// verified: `ok(stream)` *is* the authentication statement, and
        /// unverified plaintext is never observable. Any failure — a bad
        /// tag, wrong associated data, or input too short to carry the wire
        /// format — reports `error.authentication-failed` with no detail.
        open: async func(aad: list<u8>, sealed: stream<u8>) -> result<stream<u8>, error>;

        /// The registry name of the algorithm family this key is bound to,
        /// e.g. `"AES-GCM"` (spelled as for `aead-key.algorithm-name`).
        algorithm-name: func() -> string;

        /// The key length in bits, e.g. `256`.
        algorithm-length: func() -> u32;

        /// The number of further `seal` invocations this key's nonce budget
        /// permits, or `none` when the implementation enforces no budget.
        /// A hint for key-rotation scheduling: monotonically non-increasing,
        /// and `some(0)` means the next `seal` fails `error.key-exhausted` —
        /// but implementations MAY decrement faster than one per seal (a
        /// policy or accounting choice), so it is not an exact invocation
        /// count.
        ///
        /// There are deliberately no wire-layout getters here (nonce or tag
        /// size): the sealed message's layout is the minting interface's
        /// contract, and a consumer parsing it already knows that
        /// interface's documented constants.
        seals-remaining: func() -> option<u64>;

        /// Whether the key material may be exported. See `mac-key`.
        extractable: func() -> bool;

        /// The raw key material. Fails with `error.not-extractable` unless
        /// the key was created with `extractable` true. The nonce budget
        /// does not travel with the material — see the minting interfaces'
        /// import docs.
        export-key: async func() -> result<list<u8>, error>;
    }
}

/// The `digest` primitive kind: cryptographic hash functions.
///
/// `mac` minus keys: there is no key material, so no extractability and no
/// export. The stateful object is the `digest` resource — an algorithm-bound
/// capability minted by per-algorithm interfaces (such as `sha2`) — so a
/// component whose world imports only this interface can use any digest it
/// is granted but cannot choose algorithms.
interface digest {
    use types.{error};

    /// A digest algorithm, bound at creation.
    ///
    /// `compute` is one-shot and stateless per call (the resource is
    /// reusable), mirroring `crypto.subtle.digest`: there is deliberately no
    /// incremental computation resource, so misuse of in-progress state is
    /// unrepresentable. The byte `stream` is the only ingestion path and
    /// also the incremental one; the result is chunking-invariant. See
    /// `mac-key` for the truncating-producer note, which applies here too.
    ///
    /// A digest authenticates nothing by itself. There is deliberately no
    /// `verify` here: checking untrusted data against a known digest is an
    /// *integrity* comparison, done with `bytes.constant-time-equal` on
    /// `compute`'s result — when authenticity is needed, use a `mac-key`.
    resource digest {
        /// Digest an entire byte stream. Resolves once the stream is fully
        /// drained (its writer dropped).
        ///
        /// Fails only for operational reasons (`error.other`, e.g. an
        /// external digest engine backing this resource that cannot
        /// complete the operation) — never for misuse, which is
        /// unrepresentable.
        compute: async func(data: stream<u8>) -> result<list<u8>, error>;

        /// The registry name of the algorithm this resource is bound to,
        /// e.g. `"SHA-256"` (as `crypto.subtle.digest` spells it). For a
        /// digest the algorithm *is* the hash, so there is no separate
        /// `algorithm-hash` getter.
        algorithm-name: func() -> string;
    }
}

/// Utility operations on byte strings.
interface bytes {
    /// Whether `a` and `b` are equal, in time independent of their
    /// *contents* (necessarily dependent on their lengths). Use this to
    /// compare a computed digest or tag against an untrusted expected value
    /// without creating a timing oracle.
    ///
    /// Returns a `bool`, not a `result`: unlike `mac-key.verify`, a byte
    /// comparison is not an authentication statement, and the caller decides
    /// what equality means.
    constant-time-equal: func(a: list<u8>, b: list<u8>) -> bool;
}

/// The `signature` primitive kind: asymmetric signing and verification.
///
/// This interface is algorithm-agnostic. Keys are minted by per-algorithm
/// interfaces and returned bound to their algorithm at creation; every
/// operation hangs off a key resource, so a key can never be used with the
/// wrong algorithm.
///
/// The public and private halves are distinct resource types, split by
/// secrecy: a component holding only a `verifying-key` provably cannot
/// sign. Minting is likewise split into per-algorithm `-verify` and `-sign`
/// interfaces, so a provider can serve verification for an algorithm whose
/// signing it declines to host — verification is secret-free, while signing
/// may be excluded by the in-guest provider's timing-channel policy (see
/// guest-impl/README.md, class D) at `wac plug` time.
///
/// There is deliberately no way to derive a `verifying-key` from a
/// `signing-key`: the public half is available where it is guaranteed to
/// exist — `generate-key` returns the pair — and importers supply it via
/// `import-verifying-key`. A provider may hold a private key whose public
/// half it cannot recompute (browser WebCrypto has no derive operation and
/// engines diverge on recovering the point from private-only imports;
/// keystore-resident non-extractable keys can sign but yield nothing
/// else), so a derive contract here would make such keys unservable.
///
/// See `mac-key` for the truncating-producer and making-progress notes,
/// which apply to `sign` and `verify` here too.
interface signature {
    use types.{error};

    /// A public key: verification only, secret-free.
    resource verifying-key {
        /// Verify `sig` over an entire byte stream. Like `mac-key.verify`,
        /// resolves only after the stream is fully drained — even when
        /// verification fails — and fails closed with
        /// `error.authentication-failed` (deliberately not a `bool`: an
        /// ignored boolean fails open, a dropped `result` does not).
        ///
        /// Signature verification is a *policy*, not a bit-exact predicate:
        /// the precise criterion — which degenerate keys and signatures
        /// must be rejected — is defined by the key's minting interface,
        /// exactly like the wire format.
        verify: async func(data: stream<u8>, sig: list<u8>) -> result<_, error>;

        /// The registry name of the algorithm family this key is bound to,
        /// e.g. `"Ed25519"` or `"ECDSA"` (WebCrypto's `KeyAlgorithm.name`).
        algorithm-name: func() -> string;

        /// The registry name of the curve for algorithms parameterized by
        /// one, e.g. `"P-256"` (WebCrypto's `EcKeyAlgorithm.namedCurve`).
        /// `none` for Ed25519, whose curve is implied by the name.
        algorithm-curve: func() -> option<string>;

        /// The digest bound at mint, e.g. `"SHA-256"`. `none` for Ed25519:
        /// RFC 8032 fixes SHA-512 internally, so it is not a parameter.
        algorithm-hash: func() -> option<string>;

        /// The public key material, in the minting interface's documented
        /// public format.
        ///
        /// There is no extractability gate on this resource, so
        /// `error.not-extractable` never occurs here. Export is still
        /// fallible: a provider may hold the key as a handle it can *use*
        /// but not *read* — verifying with it succeeds while recovering its
        /// encoding fails with `error.other`. Browser WebCrypto draws the
        /// same line, giving every export operation a "key material cannot
        /// be accessed" failure that sign and verify do not have.
        export-key: async func() -> result<list<u8>, error>;
    }

    /// A private key. `sign` is one-shot on the immutable key, mirroring
    /// `mac-key.sign`; there is no incremental computation resource.
    ///
    /// Note on extractability: `extractable` is an API property, not a
    /// physical one — see `mac-key`.
    resource signing-key {
        /// Sign an entire byte stream. Resolves once the stream is fully
        /// drained (its writer dropped). The signature's wire format is
        /// documented by the minting interface.
        ///
        /// Fails only for operational reasons (`error.other`, e.g. an
        /// external keystore backing this key that cannot complete the
        /// operation) — never for misuse, which is unrepresentable.
        sign: async func(data: stream<u8>) -> result<list<u8>, error>;

        /// See `verifying-key.algorithm-name`.
        algorithm-name: func() -> string;

        /// See `verifying-key.algorithm-curve`.
        algorithm-curve: func() -> option<string>;

        /// See `verifying-key.algorithm-hash`.
        algorithm-hash: func() -> option<string>;

        /// Whether the private key material may be exported.
        extractable: func() -> bool;

        /// The private key material, in the minting interface's documented
        /// private format. Fails with `error.not-extractable` unless the
        /// key was created with `extractable` true.
        export-key: async func() -> result<list<u8>, error>;
    }
}

